<ContentLayout structuredData={__sd} title="Extension cord getting hot: stop use and diagnose the cause | StackGeist" description="A hot extension cord is a warning sign. Stop use, check load, damage, plug and outlet condition, and replace or correct the setup before reconnecting equipment." active="guides">
<article class="article-page">
<p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / Hot extension cord</p>
<p class="page-kicker">Electrical safety</p>
<h1 class="page-title">Extension cord getting hot: stop using it and find the cause.</h1>
<p class="page-lead">Heat is not a performance quirk to work around. It can come from overload, conductor resistance, damaged insulation/connections, or a poor plug/receptacle contact. Unplug first, then diagnose.</p>

<div class="article-summary"><strong>The five-minute answer</strong><p>Disconnect the extension cord if it is safe to do so. Let it cool while unplugged. Compare the connected load with the cord’s marked rating and the equipment instructions. Inspect the entire cord, plug, sockets, and wall outlet for discoloration, melting, looseness, crushing, or cracked insulation. Replace damaged cords; do not tape or splice them back into desk service.</p></div>

<div class="article-body">
<section><p class="page-kicker">Safety gate</p><h2>Hot, smoking, melting, sparking, or burning-smell means stop use.</h2>
<p>CPSC warns that extension cords can overheat when overloaded or damaged and that hot plugs/sockets can be associated with deteriorated connections. If there is smoke, active arcing, melting, or a fire hazard, disconnect power only if it can be done safely and use emergency services as appropriate.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 01</p><h2>Calculate what the cord is actually carrying.</h2>
<p>Use equipment labels or power-supply ratings to estimate the maximum simultaneous load and compare it with the extension cord’s marked amp/watt rating. Do not assume a cord is adequate because the connected devices normally draw less than their adapter’s maximum.</p>
<p>If a device manual says it should not be used on an extension cord, follow the appliance manufacturer’s instruction.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 02</p><h2>Use the heat location as a clue, not proof.</h2>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>Whole cord warm/hot</strong><p>Load, wire gauge/length, damage, or bundled/covered routing can contribute.</p></div>
<div><strong>One plug/socket hot</strong><p>A loose or resistive connection becomes more concerning. Inspect both mating parts.</p></div>
<div><strong>Wall receptacle hot or loose</strong><p>Stop using that receptacle for the setup and have the building wiring/receptacle evaluated.</p></div>
</div></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 03</p><h2>Check routing and physical condition.</h2>
<p>Do not run the cord under rugs or where furniture, doors, chair casters, or cable-management hardware crushes it. Do not coil or cover a heavily loaded cord in a way that traps heat. Replace cords with cut, cracked, abraded, melted, or loose components.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Decision</p><h2>What fixes the problem?</h2>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>Load problem</strong><p>Reduce the load or use an appropriate permanent circuit/receptacle arrangement.</p></div>
<div><strong>Cord problem</strong><p>Replace it with a listed cord whose rating and environment match the intended temporary use.</p></div>
<div><strong>Outlet problem</strong><p>Stop using that outlet and involve a qualified electrician.</p></div>
</div></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Related</p><div class="cards">
